---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import AspectRatioCalculator from '@/components/tools/units/AspectRatioCalculator.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="aspect-ratio-calculator">
  <AspectRatioCalculator client:load mode="aspect" />

  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>The whole calculation, once</h2>
    <p>
      An aspect ratio is one number: width divided by height. An aspect ratio calculator uses two
      rearrangements of it.
    </p>
    <pre><code>ratio  = width ÷ height
height = width ÷ ratio
width  = height × ratio</code></pre>
    <p>
      Type into the width box and the tool solves the height; type into the height box and it solves
      the width. The page shows the arithmetic with your numbers, so you can check it by hand.
    </p>

    <h2>Simplifying a ratio</h2>
    <p>
      To reduce 1920 × 1080 to 16:9, divide both sides by their greatest common divisor. Euclid's
      algorithm finds it in a few steps: gcd(1920, 1080) → gcd(1080, 840) → gcd(840, 240) →
      gcd(240, 120) → gcd(120, 0) = 120. Then 1920 ÷ 120 = 16 and 1080 ÷ 120 = 9. Dimensions that do
      not reduce to small numbers, such as 1366 × 768 (683:384), show as a decimal ratio, because a
      large integer pair is not useful.
    </p>

    <h2>Ratios worth knowing</h2>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Ratio</th><th>Decimal</th><th>Where it is used</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>16:9</td><td>1.778</td><td>HD and 4K video, most monitors and televisions, YouTube</td></tr>
          <tr><td>9:16</td><td>0.563</td><td>Stories, Reels, Shorts, TikTok</td></tr>
          <tr><td>4:5</td><td>0.800</td><td>Instagram portrait, the tallest shape allowed in the feed</td></tr>
          <tr><td>1:1</td><td>1.000</td><td>Square posts, avatars, album art</td></tr>
          <tr><td>4:3</td><td>1.333</td><td>Classic television, iPad, Micro Four Thirds, most projectors</td></tr>
          <tr><td>3:2</td><td>1.500</td><td>35 mm film and almost all DSLR and mirrorless sensors</td></tr>
          <tr><td>64:27 (“21:9”)</td><td>2.370</td><td>Ultrawide monitors: 2560 × 1080 and 3440 × 1440</td></tr>
          <tr><td>2.39:1</td><td>2.390</td><td>Anamorphic cinema scope</td></tr>
          <tr><td>√2:1</td><td>1.414</td><td>ISO paper, A0 to A6, so half of A4 is A5</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h2>Why “21:9” is not 21:9</h2>
    <p>
      A literal 21:9 is 2.333. No ultrawide panel has that shape: 2560 × 1080 and 3440 × 1440 both
      reduce to 64:27, which is 2.370. The name looks good next to “16:9” on a spec sheet. The preset
      button on this page uses the real 64:27, so its dimensions match the hardware. The same applies
      to “18:9” phones (really 2:1) and “19.5:9” (2.167).
    </p>

    <h2>Common resolutions at a glance</h2>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Resolution</th><th>Ratio</th><th>Megapixels</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>1280 × 720</td><td>16:9</td><td>0.92</td></tr>
          <tr><td>1920 × 1080</td><td>16:9</td><td>2.07</td></tr>
          <tr><td>2560 × 1440</td><td>16:9</td><td>3.69</td></tr>
          <tr><td>3840 × 2160</td><td>16:9</td><td>8.29</td></tr>
          <tr><td>1080 × 1350</td><td>4:5</td><td>1.46</td></tr>
          <tr><td>1080 × 1920</td><td>9:16</td><td>2.07</td></tr>
          <tr><td>1024 × 768</td><td>4:3</td><td>0.79</td></tr>
          <tr><td>3440 × 1440</td><td>64:27</td><td>4.95</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h2>Letterboxing, pillarboxing and cropping</h2>
    <p>
      When the source and destination ratios differ, there are three choices. Fit keeps everything and
      adds bars: horizontal bars (letterboxing) when the source is wider, vertical bars (pillarboxing)
      when it is taller. Fill covers the frame and crops the overflow. Stretch distorts the image and
      is almost never correct. Calculate the loss before you decide: 16:9 video in a 9:16 frame needs
      bars over 68% of the height, so vertical video is usually shot again, not converted.
    </p>

    <h2>Megapixels</h2>
    <p>
      Megapixels are width × height ÷ 1,000,000, and they scale with the square of the linear size.
      Two times both dimensions gives four times the pixels, and about four times the memory the
      browser needs for the image. Remember this before you make a collage from twenty 24-megapixel
      photos.
    </p>

    <h2>Related tools</h2>
    <p>
      <a href="/screen-size-calculator">Screen Size Calculator</a> turns a diagonal into real width
      and height, <a href="/resize-image">Resize Image</a> applies the dimensions you calculated
      here, <a href="/crop-video">Crop Video</a> has the social ratios as presets, and
      <a href="/combine-images">Combine Images</a> helps when you know the output shape.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
